# /srv/atlas/secrets/mcp-reader.env — SCOPED secrets for the read-only MCP-reader service.
#
# Copy this file to /srv/atlas/secrets/mcp-reader.env on the production box.
# Set mode 600: chmod 600 /srv/atlas/secrets/mcp-reader.env
# This file is read by docker-compose.prod.yml via the `mcp-reader` service's `env_file`.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE FILE (not atlas.env): the reader is the single controlled crossing point between the
# isolated sandbox network and the trusted data plane, and it ingests UNTRUSTED, prompt-injectable data.
# Its read-only posture is enforced structurally by the SELECT-only `mcp_reader` Postgres role. It MUST
# therefore never see the backend's full secret bundle — no full-privilege POSTGRES_PASSWORD, JWT_*,
# SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY, or GitHub tokens — or a compromised reader could connect to Postgres as the
# read/write role and bypass the SELECT-only role entirely. Keep ONLY the MCP_READER_* values below here.
#
# The non-secret wiring (MCP_READER_PORT, MCP_READER_PG_HOST/PORT/SSL, AGENT_HOME_ROOT/REPOS_ROOT) is set
# in the compose `environment:` block, not here.

# Static key the Atlas-repo sandbox's MCP client sends as `x-api-key`. Generate a long random value
# (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`). Register the SAME value in the web UI MCP settings for the Atlas repo
# ONLY (transport Streamable HTTP, URL http://mcp-reader:4100/, header X-Api-Key: <this>).
# ROTATION: change it here AND re-save the web-registry value, then `docker compose up -d mcp-reader`.
MCP_READER_API_KEY=CHANGEME
# The SELECT-only DB role (see infra/mcp-reader-role.sql). MCP_READER_PG_DB MUST equal POSTGRES_DB.
MCP_READER_PG_USER=mcp_reader
MCP_READER_PG_PASSWORD=CHANGEME
MCP_READER_PG_DB=CHANGEME
